CH = dict(num=10, title="Tissues in Action", tag="Biology", theme="Plant and animal tissues, joints and the skeletal system",
 intro="Cells of the same kind group into tissues, giving division of labour and efficiency. This chapter classifies plant tissues (meristematic and permanent) and animal tissues (epithelial, connective, muscular, nervous), then links bones, muscles, joints and the skeleton to movement.",
 big_questions=["How are tissues in plants and animals different, and why?", "Why do some tissues grow throughout life while others do not?", "How is the structure of a tissue matched to its function?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "10.1 Tissues and Why Plants and Animals Differ"),
 ("ul", ["A **tissue** is a group of cells, similar in structure, that work together for a specific function. Organisation: cells → tissues → organs → organ systems → organism. Unicellular organisms like *Amoeba* do everything in one cell; multicellular ones show **division of labour**, which raises efficiency.", "Animal examples: muscle tissue moves the body, nervous tissue carries messages. Plant examples: **xylem** carries water and minerals, **phloem** carries food.", "**Why plant and animal tissues differ**: plants are fixed in place and need rigidity (cell walls and supporting tissues); animals move and have flexible cells. Plants make food by photosynthesis, animals digest it. Transport and growth patterns also differ (plant growth is localised in meristems)."]),
 ("h2", "10.2 Tissues for Growth in Plants — Meristematic Tissue"),
 ("p", "Plants grow in length, in girth and by regrowth after cutting or grazing. This needs actively dividing cells, forming **meristematic tissue**."),
 ("steps", [("Activity 10.1 — onion roots", "Place two onion bulbs over water-filled jars and measure root length on days 1–3."), ("Cut", "On day 3 cut about 1 cm off the root tips in Jar B and keep measuring."), ("Result", "Roots in Jar A keep growing; Jar B roots stop. Hence roots grow from their tips, where cells divide continuously (growth zone).")]),
 ("table", ["Meristem", "Location", "Gives growth in", "Examples / notes"], [
   ["**Apical**", "Tips of roots and shoots", "Length (height, root depth)", "Root tip of onion; sapling"],
   ["**Lateral**", "Ring of dividing cells in the stem circumference", "Girth (thickness); makes new cells inside and outside concentrically", "Dicot stems; **annual rings** in a trunk"],
   ["**Intercalary**", "Base of internode / just above the node", "Regrowth and branching after cutting", "Grass after mowing or grazing; hedges becoming bushy"]]),
 ("ul", ["**Annual rings**: wide ring = favourable year, narrow = unfavourable. Counting them gives the tree's age and past climate (a teak of 40 years shows 40 rings).", "**Node** is where leaves or branches arise; **internode** lies between two nodes. If a young stem tip is cut, length growth stops but new branches arise at nodes.", "**Features of meristematic cells**: small, thin walls, large prominent nucleus, dense cytoplasm with many organelles, **no vacuoles**, tightly packed with no intercellular spaces — suited to fast, continuous division.", "**Differentiation**: some new cells lose the power to divide and become specialised; this converts meristematic into **permanent tissue**."]),
 ("h2", "10.3 Permanent Tissues"),
 ("p", "Permanent tissues may be **simple** (one type of cell) or **complex** (more than one type of cell). Functionally they protect, support or conduct. A sunflower stem T.S. shows epidermis, ground tissue (parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma) and vascular tissue (xylem and phloem)."),
 ("h3", "Protective tissue — epidermis"),
 ("ul", ["Outermost layer: a single layer of tightly packed, flat rectangular cells, coated with waxy **cutin** forming the **cuticle**. It guards against injury, water loss, parasites and extreme conditions; desert plants have a thicker cuticle to cut transpiration.", "**Root hairs** (epidermal projections) increase the surface for absorbing water and minerals.", "In leaves the epidermis has **stomata** for gas exchange and **transpiration** (loss of water vapour), which creates a transpiration pull in xylem and removes wastes.", "In older stems, cells below the epidermis form **cork cambium** (a lateral meristem) that makes **cork cells** — dead, compact and impermeable to water and gases — which form the **bark**."]),
 ("h3", "Supporting tissue — simple permanent tissues"),
 ("table", ["Tissue", "Cells and wall", "Living?", "Function / where"], [
   ["**Parenchyma**", "Thin walls, loosely packed with intercellular spaces", "Living", "Stores food; photosynthesis in green parts (chlorenchyma); air spaces help aquatic plants float"],
   ["**Collenchyma**", "Corners unevenly thickened with **pectin**", "Living", "Support with **flexibility**; stems, leaf stalks, tendrils bend without breaking"],
   ["**Sclerenchyma**", "Thick walls hardened with **lignin**", "Mostly dead", "Hardness and strength; woody parts, leaf veins, coconut husk fibres, seed coats, walnut shell"]]),
 ("h3", "Conducting tissue — complex permanent tissues (vascular)"),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Xylem", "Phloem"], [
   ["Carries", "Water and minerals from roots upward", "Food (sugars) from leaves to all parts"],
   ["Components", "Tracheids, vessels, xylem parenchyma, xylem fibres", "Sieve tubes, companion cells, phloem parenchyma, phloem fibres"],
   ["Living / dead", "Mostly dead (only xylem parenchyma is living); tubular, thick-walled", "Mostly living"],
   ["Extra role", "Gives mechanical strength", "Companion cells regulate sieve tubes and load/unload sugars; parenchyma stores food, resin, tannins and latex"]]),
 ("p", "Sieve tubes are long cells joined end to end by perforated walls (sieve plates). Fibres in both tissues are sclerenchymatous and give strength."),
 ("table", ["Tissue system", "Made of", "Role"], [["Dermal", "Epidermis (and cork)", "Outer covering, protection, reduces water loss"], ["Ground", "Parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma", "Main body between dermal and vascular tissue"], ["Vascular", "Xylem and phloem", "Conduction"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "Meristematic tissues divide; permanent tissues have lost this ability. Plant tissue types: **meristematic** (apical, lateral, intercalary) and **permanent** (simple: parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma; complex: xylem, phloem; protective: epidermis, cork)."),
 ("h2", "10.4 Animal Tissues"),
 ("p", "Animal tissues are of four main kinds: **epithelial, connective, muscular and nervous**."),
 ("h3", "Epithelial tissue"),
 ("p", "Covers the body (skin) and lines internal organs (mouth, lungs, blood vessels, intestine). Cells are packed tightly with little space, blocking germs, reducing water loss and aiding absorption, secretion and movement of substances."),
 ("table", ["Function", "Structure", "Location"], [
   ["Exchange (rapid diffusion of gases/liquids)", "Single layer of thin, flat cells", "Blood vessels, lungs (alveoli)"],
   ["Protection from injury, friction, microbes", "Many layers; outer cells flat and tight", "Skin, mouth, oesophagus"],
   ["Secretion (mucus, enzymes, hormones, sweat, saliva)", "Cuboidal or columnar gland cells", "Salivary glands, sweat glands, stomach lining"],
   ["Sensing (smell, taste, sound, balance)", "Receptor cells with hair-like cilia", "Nostrils, taste buds, inner ear"],
   ["Absorption of nutrients and water", "Single layer of tall, pillar-like cells, often with hair-like projections", "Small intestine"]]),
 ("h3", "Connective tissue"),
 ("p", "Connects and supports other tissues. The **matrix** (the non-cellular material) decides its nature: watery and jelly-like in blood, hard and rigid in bone."),
 ("table", ["Tissue", "Matrix / nature", "Function"], [
   ["**Blood**", "Fluid plasma (about 55% of volume) with formed elements — RBC, WBC, platelets (45%)", "Transports nutrients, gases, hormones"],
   ["**Bone**", "Hard, rigid matrix with calcium and phosphorus compounds", "Strength, support, protection"],
   ["**Cartilage**", "Soft, jelly-like, flexible (ear, nose tip, rib ends, between vertebrae)", "Flexibility; cushions bone ends against shock"],
   ["**Tendon**", "Tough flexible band", "Joins **muscle to bone**; transmits force"],
   ["**Ligament**", "Tough, slightly elastic band", "Joins **bone to bone**; stabilises joint, limits movement, prevents dislocation"]]),
 ("table", ["Blood component", "Role"], [["RBC (haemoglobin, an iron-rich protein)", "Red colour; carries oxygen; lives about 4 months"], ["Platelets", "Blood clotting at a cut"], ["WBC", "Gather at infection, causing pus, redness and swelling; defence"]]),
 ("h3", "Muscular tissue"),
 ("table", ["Muscle", "Control", "Cell features", "Location / action"], [
   ["**Skeletal**", "Voluntary", "Long cylindrical, unbranched, **multinucleate**, **striated** fibres", "Attached to skeleton; running, writing, lifting"],
   ["**Smooth**", "Involuntary", "Spindle-shaped, single nucleus, **no striations**", "Stomach, intestine; slow continuous movement (digestion)"],
   ["**Cardiac**", "Involuntary", "Cylindrical, **branched**, one nucleus, faint striations", "Heart only; beats rhythmically throughout life without fatigue"]]),
 ("h3", "Nervous tissue"),
 ("p", "Made of **neurons**, specialised to receive, process and transmit messages; the brain is the control centre. Muscles need instructions from nervous tissue (e.g. the brain makes the heart beat faster in exercise)."),
 ("table", ["Part of neuron", "Function"], [["Cell body", "Contains the nucleus; controls the cell"], ["Dendrites", "Receive signals from other neurons"], ["Axon", "Long fibre carrying the message away; ends in axon terminals that pass it on to other cells"]]),
 ("h2", "10.5 Musculoskeletal System and Joints"),
 ("ul", ["Made of bones, muscles, joints, cartilage, tendons and ligaments; helps us stand, move, hold posture and protect organs; it works under the **nervous system**. Muscles pull on bones via tendons, producing movement at a joint.", "The adult skeleton is about **12–15%** of body weight; muscle about 40–50% in men and 30–40% in women (approximate)."]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Joint**: the junction between two or more bones. Joints permit movement, but cannot move bones themselves — muscles do."),
 ("table", ["Joint", "Movement allowed", "Example"], [
   ["**Ball and socket**", "Free: forward, backward, sideways, circular", "Shoulder (round head of upper arm bone fits in hollow of shoulder bone); hip"],
   ["**Hinge**", "One direction only, like a door hinge", "Elbow; knee (kneecap protects)"],
   ["**Pivot**", "Side-to-side rotation", "Skull on backbone (head turning 'no')"],
   ["**Fixed**", "No movement", "Bones of the skull, protecting brain, eyes and ears"]]),
 ("h2", "10.6 Skeletal System"),
 ("ul", ["Framework of bones for strength and protection: **skull, vertebral column and rib cage**.", "The **backbone (spine)** is a flexible column of **vertebrae**, with cartilage discs between them that cushion, give flexibility for bending and twisting and protect the spinal cord.", "**Rib cage**: 12 pairs of ribs joined at the back to the spine and in front to the **sternum** by flexible cartilage. It protects heart and lungs and expands and contracts during breathing.", "Good posture, nutrition, exercise and **yoga** (International Yoga Day, 21 June) keep bones strong, muscles fit and joints flexible."]),
 ("h2", "10.7 Totipotency and Other Extensions"),
 ("ul", ["**F. C. Steward (1958)** grew single phloem cells of carrot in a nutrient medium with sugars and hormones; they divided into a cell mass (dedifferentiation) and then redifferentiated into roots, shoots and a whole plant. Such cells are **totipotent**, like a zygote.", "Cork cambium and cork also arise in old stems (see protective tissue).", "**Stem cells** in bone marrow divide to make new cells; bone marrow transplants help patients with leukemia and thalassemia.", "Crown gall disease (tumour-like swelling) is caused by *Agrobacterium tumefaciens*; its gene transfer is now used in genetic engineering of plants."]),
 ("h2", "10.8 Quick Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
   ("What is a tissue? Why is division of labour useful?", "A group of similar cells working together for a function; specialisation makes complex life processes efficient."),
   ("Why do plants have more supporting tissue than animals?", "They are fixed in one place and must stand upright against wind and rain; animals move and have flexible cells."),
   ("Name the three types of meristem with location and function.", "Apical: tips, length; lateral: along the stem circumference, girth; intercalary: base of internodes/nodes, regrowth."),
   ("Why do meristematic cells lack vacuoles?", "They need dense cytoplasm and a large nucleus for fast division; vacuoles would crowd them."),
   ("How do plant growth rings form and what do they tell?", "Lateral meristem adds new cells yearly; the number of rings gives age and the width shows climate of each year."),
   ("Differentiate parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma.", "Parenchyma: thin-walled living storage cells; collenchyma: pectin-thickened corners, flexible support; sclerenchyma: lignified, dead, hard."),
   ("Why can a fresh twig bend but a dry twig snaps?", "Collenchyma keeps the fresh twig flexible; dry twigs rely on rigid lignified tissue."),
   ("Compare xylem and phloem.", "Xylem carries water and minerals upwards and is mostly dead; phloem carries food and is mostly living with sieve tubes and companion cells."),
   ("What is the function of stomata and cuticle?", "Stomata allow gas exchange and transpiration; cuticle reduces water loss and protects."),
   ("Why are RBCs red and what do platelets do?", "Haemoglobin (iron-rich) makes them red; platelets help clotting."),
   ("Differentiate tendon and ligament.", "Tendon joins muscle to bone; ligament joins bone to bone."),
   ("Compare skeletal, smooth and cardiac muscle.", "Skeletal: voluntary, striated, multinucleate; smooth: involuntary, spindle, no striations; cardiac: involuntary, branched, heart only."),
   ("Name the parts of a neuron.", "Cell body, dendrites and axon with axon terminals."),
   ("Name four types of joints with an example each.", "Ball and socket (shoulder), hinge (elbow/knee), pivot (neck-skull), fixed (skull)."),
   ("What is totipotency?", "Ability of a single cell to grow into a complete organism, as shown with carrot phloem cells by Steward."),
 ])],
 takeaways=["Tissues are groups of similar cells; **plants** have **meristematic** (apical, lateral, intercalary) and **permanent** tissues.", "Permanent tissues: **epidermis** (protection), **parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma** (support), **xylem and phloem** (conduction).", "Animal tissues: **epithelial, connective (blood, bone, cartilage, tendon, ligament), muscular (skeletal, smooth, cardiac), nervous (neuron)**.", "**Tendon**: muscle–bone; **ligament**: bone–bone.", "Joints: **ball and socket, hinge, pivot, fixed**; the skeleton comprises skull, backbone and rib cage.", "**Totipotent** plant cells (Steward, carrot) can regenerate a whole plant."])
